Phase 1: High-Level Vulkan SetupBefore you even touch the graphics pipeline, you need the foundational engine components:
1. VkInstance: Initializes the Vulkan library and driver context.  
2. Physical & Logical Devices (VkPhysicalDevice, VkDevice): Queries available GPUs and creates a logical interface to submit work to queue families (Graphics, Compute, Present).  
3. Surface & Swapchain (VkSurfaceKHR, VkSwapchainKHR): Connects Vulkan to your windowing system (GLFW, SDL) and creates the frame buffers/images that hold rendered frames for display.  
4. Command Buffers & Sync Objects: Allocates command buffers where draw commands are recorded, alongside fences and semaphores to manage async CPU-GPU execution.  

Phase 2:When you build a VkGraphicsPipelineCreateInfo object, you configure the sequence of programmable and fixed-function stages.

1. Input Assembly (Fixed-Function)What it does: Reads raw vertex data from your vertex buffers.  Your Job: Tell Vulkan how to interpret the vertices (e.g., triangle list, line strip, point list) and provide indexing rules (VkPipelineInputAssemblyStateCreateInfo).  
2. Vertex Shader (Programmable)What it does: Executes once for every vertex. Converts 3D position coordinates (Model/View/Projection space) into normalized device coordinates (NDC).  Your Job: Write a SPIR-V compiled GLSL/HLSL shader module (VkShaderModule).Optional Stages:Tessellation Shaders: Dynamically subdivides geometry on the GPU for added detail.  Geometry Shader: Transforms or creates new primitives on the fly (though often skipped due to performance considerations).  Mesh Shaders (Modern Vulkan): Bypasses the traditional Input Assembly / Vertex stages for cluster-based geometry processing.
3. Rasterization (Fixed-Function)What it does: Takes geometric primitives (triangles) and breaks them down into fragments (pixels that need to be colored). It handles back-face culling, clipping primitives outside the view volume, and viewport scaling.  Your Job: Define polygon fill mode (solid vs. wireframe), face culling rules, front-face winding order, and depth bias (VkPipelineRasterizationStateCreateInfo).  
4. Fragment Shader (Programmable)What it does: Runs on every fragment that passes rasterization. Calculates the final color output, handles texture sampling, applies lighting calculations, and writes out depth values.  Your Job: Write a fragment shader compiled to SPIR-V.
5. Depth, Stencil & Color Blending (Fixed-Function)What it does: Performs depth testing (discarding fragments hidden behind other geometry) and blends the output fragment color with whatever is already sitting in the swapchain/framebuffer image.  Your Job: Configure write masks, depth comparisons (VK_COMPARE_OP_LESS), and blending equations (e.g., standard alpha blending).  